<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kazakhstan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Kazakhstan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kazakhstan is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, partly extending into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. After the Mongol conquest in the 13th century and the disintegration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, the Kazakh Khanate arose in the 15th century. By the 18th century it had fragmented into three jüz, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> gradually absorbed, and by the mid-19th century all of Kazakhstan was nominally under Russian rule. It became an autonomous republic of the Russian SFSR after the Russian Revolution, and a union republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1936. It declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 25 October 1990 and full independence on 16 December 1991, the last Soviet republic to do so.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kazakhstan.jpg" alt="Kazakhstan" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shaken Niyazbekop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kazakhstan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>There he established his own ulus, the core of what later Russian sources called the Golden Horde, while his elder brother Orda led the eastern wing in what is now <b>Kazakhstan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By bringing much of the steppe between the Caspian Sea, the Altai and the Syr Darya under Kazakh khans, the khanate formed the space on which the borders of the Kazakh Soviet Socialist Republic, and in 1991 of independent <b>Kazakhstan</b>, were later drawn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Forced collectivisation of agriculture from 1929, the deportation of so-called kulaks and the famine of 1932–1933, which struck the Volga, the North Caucasus and <b>Kazakhstan</b> as well as Ukraine, caused millions of deaths, and the Gulag camp system spread across the north and east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…usually recognised from the eighth and seventh centuries BCE in cultures such as the Tasmola culture of central <b>Kazakhstan</b> and the early Saka of Zhetysu (Semirechye), while the Aldy-Bel culture of Tuva, whose royal kurgan Arzhan 2 held thousands of gold objects, belongs to the same early Scythian…</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Industrial output grew rapidly, with new cities such as Magnitogorsk, but collectivisation destroyed much of the peasant economy and caused the famine of 1932–1933, which killed several million people, above all in Ukraine, where it is known as the Holodomor, and in <b>Kazakhstan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…1991 and the United Nations on 2 March 1992, and belongs to the OSCE, the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, the Organisation of Turkic States and the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation; it joined GUAM in 1999 and left in 2005, and founded the Central Asian Union with <b>Kazakhstan</b> and Kyrgyzstan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
